{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=4{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-x}-5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5+\\frac{25}{4})\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{16}{49}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-8103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-5x/2}+B{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{8}{49}{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-5x/2}+B{e}^{5x/2}-\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}-\\frac{4}{49}{e}^{-x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $12{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+4(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-8+8)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}={e}^{-2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-9\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+18y=8{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{6x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =6.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-9(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+18(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4-18+18)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-64289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=4{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=20{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=16\\alpha {e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha {e}^{4x}-4(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})+5(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =20{e}^{4x} \\\\ (16-16+5)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =20{e}^{4x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =20{e}^{4x} \\\\ 5\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=P{e}^{2x}+Q{e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y=P{e}^{x}+Qx{e}^{x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+5x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-2{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{e}^{4x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is ${e}^{-3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+3(-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9-9-4)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-64290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}={e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=10{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}-3(\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}) & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ (x-2-3+3x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-5x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-5{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}+3(\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (x-2+3-3x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+4x{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+2x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+4x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}-4{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+4x{e}^{-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}-2(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9x+6-2-6x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+8x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}-2x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}-6{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=2{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2{e}^{-4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-4$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (16x-8){e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (16x-8){e}^{-4x}+6(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+8(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}) & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (16x-8+6-24x+8x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ -2\\alpha  & =2 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-64292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=-x{e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=6{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}-5(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}) & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9x+6-5-15x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=3{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x},$ we have that $\\lambda =2$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3{e}^{2x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4{x}^{2}+8x+2){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4{x}^{2}+8x+2){e}^{2x}-4(\\alpha (2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x})+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+8x+2-8{x}^{2}-8x+4{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =3 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-64294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=3x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-64294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=4{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-5$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-5x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-5$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}+10(\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x})+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}) & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-20x+2+20x-50{x}^{2}+25{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=4{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-5x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=6{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x/2}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $\\frac{3}{2}$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}-3(\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2})+\\frac{9}{4}(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}) & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-6x-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-8102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x/2}+B{e}^{-x/2}+{e}^{3x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}-{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+B{e}^{-3x/2}+2x{e}^{3x/2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=6{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-3$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{-3x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9{x}^{2}-12x+2){e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9{x}^{2}-12x+2){e}^{-3x}+6(\\alpha (2x-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-3x})+9(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}) & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-12x+2+12x-18{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=4{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4{x}^{2}-8x+2){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4{x}^{2}-8x+2){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4{x}^{2}-8x+2+8x-8{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}+3x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{-2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=8{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=8{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{0}+2(0){e}^{0} \\\\ 4 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{0}+2(0){e}^{0}+2{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =-3A+B+2 \\\\ 0 & =-3A+B \\\\ B & =3A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=4 \\\\ B=3A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=3.$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=3,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{-3x}+2x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-2{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}-2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0 & =A-2 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+B{e}^{x}-4{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}-4{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0 & =A+B-4 \\\\ 0 & =2+B-4 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=2,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{-x}-3x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=4x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=-6{e}^{-2x},\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-6{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+10(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-12+10)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =-6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-3{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(Q-3P){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(P+3Q){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(P+3Q){e}^{-3\\pi /2}+6{e}^{-\\pi } & =-2{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -(P+12{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-12{e}^{-\\pi } & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ P & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4{e}^{\\pi /2}$ and $Q=4{e}^{\\pi /2}.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ y & =4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-8108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+3{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=4{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=4{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)}+4(0){e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-2B{e}^{-2x}-8x{e}^{-2x}+4{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)}-8(0){e}^{-2(0)}+4{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =-3A-2B+4 \\\\ -3 & =-3A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -3A-2B=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=0.$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=0,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-2x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-93691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=-{e}^{-3x}-2{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-93691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-12(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-2-12){e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+4B{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3A+4B-2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -3A+4B=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=1.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a8da4136543e6d2cfc498a345348163ac67e16e7525f51e0cb6a5e644f3cb2a"} {"question" "q-8100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "q-8100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fb0ca4377790cc81dfeb398b891a3ea5c092378e631f2b50be62eeb0300306b"} {"question" "e-6609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6609.json" "file_sha256" "6e0943517efc5ae44413c3c25474f2c51d3d6b8a98c9376571e1e70362357145"} {"question" "e-6609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1, -2,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9  \\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha (9+3-2){e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is \n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6609.json" "file_sha256" "6e0943517efc5ae44413c3c25474f2c51d3d6b8a98c9376571e1e70362357145"} {"question" "e-6610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/4652a17e8836f9ea84df971537f3c0df1f5ccd2b2dfd7d8d9615ef69a775b972.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6610.json" "file_sha256" "5d7ea5777f93ddf2f217f59de952302c1a787fac99bb6cc8ed4b2a2612e27ed8"} {"question" "e-6610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/bb796871deb9ebb6ff0ac3c98715a74275b8957374753a1bfed998898de136f8.png) we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/fbd5028588b1b3639ae465a5904a49e354da5f77ae0224b398666449dcfd809b.png)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/4d6d063a30dc8f4bab52eab0764e8dee217ddd74055d79cae2bcca34e46ca45f.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/df86a6554579076b0783330da9971ee6ac847ce0d7615c81da959cc197372021.png) is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/187230e4c3f0192e65cb84e69fa12ece83467ae67ca3f73f09ae636d8eb7daad.png)\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/13751f284d6fa34631cd1eb362665db0f1e29de98a1539e514009e7c5034e855.png) is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/6316c498377eadd30922f7f5ef6cfa1015cb3c059349bccf2aec7ca52ea5356e.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/955d1d9ea20426db629258e32703eda1f724e0787bedc68c653eaa684a997f02.png)\n\nTo find the value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/07f02913cce959d6fde7770ff94b1fbab15d24d8c7f7ee5c8cba48873d955f86.png)  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/0770a9b23e24c99631cdbc12378388ff089585206f0aea21ebfb3f65217cd4a2.png)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/67180bb752c7046f8b83aadacd7753bed9d827516c4f1930b341873b5399951e.png)\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/dbf2415e9f21a606aa92b6e26582cc1790470343346e967a0ff621c678560fd6.png)\n\nand the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/a197e920c5e9c7d6b16fffcd7955fe07a212b92fb50636feae2c771f91a68f8a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6610.json" "file_sha256" "5d7ea5777f93ddf2f217f59de952302c1a787fac99bb6cc8ed4b2a2612e27ed8"} {"question" "e-6611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6611.json" "file_sha256" "1025dd1ad41da678cd054b88fe96a90cb0e2a5ae70ee37d06b3951c167e18e5d"} {"question" "e-6611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root $\\lambda = 3,$ the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}-6(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+9\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+12x+2-18{x}^{2}-12x+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =1 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6611.json" "file_sha256" "1025dd1ad41da678cd054b88fe96a90cb0e2a5ae70ee37d06b3951c167e18e5d"} {"question" "e-6787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\nAe^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6787.json" "file_sha256" "66b18f850607db3b54fa16484e2501cdf0f933ae76c45f6a713cd81a1c15e6d2"} {"question" "e-6787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{3x}+3Bx{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+3B(0){e}^{0}+0{e}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =3A+B \\\\ 1 & =3(2)+B \\\\ -5 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2{e}^{3x}-5x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/example-6787.json" "file_sha256" "66b18f850607db3b54fa16484e2501cdf0f933ae76c45f6a713cd81a1c15e6d2"}] "usage" {"e-6610" [] "e-6609" [] "q-64290" [] "e-6787" [] "q-93683" [] "e-6611" [] "q-8100" []} "basis" 2143 "retractions" [[17592186088172 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4y = e^{-3x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Be^{-4x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."] [17592186088394 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 4e^{3x}$."] [17592186088333 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 12y = 10e^{2x}, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 2$."] [17592186087542 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}$."] [17592186087542 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1, -2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x}, \\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9 \\alpha e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9αe^{3x} + 3αe^{3x} - 2αe^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\nα(9 + 3 - 2)e^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\n10αe^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\n10α &= 20 \\\\\nα &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} + 2e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087543 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 3y = e ^ {x}$."] [17592186087543 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1,-3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-3 x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{x}$, and $e^{x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy_{p}}{dx} = (1 + x)αe^{x}, \\frac{d^{2}y_{p}}{dx^{2}} = (2 + x)αe^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 + x)αe^{x} + (1 + x)αe^{x} - 2xαe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n(2 + x + 1 + x - 2x)αe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n3αe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n3α &= 1 \\\\\nα &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 13 x e ^x\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} + \\frac{1}{3}xe^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087544 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}$."] [17592186087544 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 3\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy_{p}}{dx} &= (3x^{2} + 2x)αe^{3x}, \\frac{d^{2}y_{p}}{dx^{2}} = (9x^{2} + 12x + 2)αe^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(9x^{2} + 12x + 2)αe^{3x} - 6(3x^{2} + 2x)αe^{3x} + 9αx^{2}e^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n(9x^{2} + 12x + 2 - 18x^{2} - 12x + 9x^{2})αe^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n2αe^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n2α &= 1 \\\\\nα &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087553 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}$\n\nhas the general solution\n$Ae^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2, \\ \\ y'(0) = 1$."] [17592186087553 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n$2|= Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} + \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}e^{0}; 2|= A$.\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n$y^{′} = 3Ae^{3x} + Be^{3x} + 3Bxe^{3x} + xe^{3x} + \\frac{3}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}$,\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n$1|= 3Ae^{0} + Be^{0} + 3B(0)e^{0} + 0e^{0} + \\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}e^{0}; 1|= 3A + B; 1|= 3(2) + B; - 5|= B$.\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5$, we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny = 2e^{3x} - 5xe^{3x} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085964 17592186085965 17592186085966 17592186085967 17592186085968 17592186086929 17592186086930 17592186086931 17592186086932 17592186086933 17592186087295 17592186087296 17592186087297 17592186087298 17592186087299] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085963 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085963 :answer-field/id] [17592186085963 :answer-field/key] [17592186085963 :answer-field/type] [17592186086928 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086928 :answer-field/id] [17592186086928 :answer-field/key] [17592186086928 :answer-field/type] [17592186087294 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087294 :answer-field/id] [17592186087294 :answer-field/key] [17592186087294 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
